        <div id="how-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How a Gas Tankless Water Heater Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best gas tankless water heater is the one sized to your household's peak hot water use and your local groundwater temperature. Understanding how these heaters work makes sizing far easier.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heating Water on Demand</h3>

          <p>
            When you open a hot tap, water flowing through the unit trips a flow sensor, a gas burner fires, and a heat exchanger heats the water as it passes through. When you close the tap, the burner stops. There is no tank of hot water cooling down between uses, which is where the savings come from. Our guide to <a href="/what-is-a-tankless-water-heater/">what a tankless water heater is</a> covers the basics in more depth.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do Gas Tankless Water Heaters Need Electricity?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes. Gas tankless units use electricity for the controls, ignition, fan and freeze protection, typically from a standard 120-volt outlet; the Noritz listing, for example, specifies 120 volts. In a power outage, a gas tankless heater will not make hot water unless it has backup power.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas vs Electric Tankless</h3>

          <p>
            Gas units deliver much more hot water than electric tankless heaters on typical home electrical service, which is why gas is the usual choice for whole-house tankless in colder climates. Our guide to the <a href="/best-electric-tankless-water-heater/">best electric tankless water heaters</a> covers the electric side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="condensing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Condensing vs Non-Condensing: The Main Choice
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gas tankless heaters come in two designs, and the choice affects efficiency, venting and installation cost.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Condensing Units</h3>

          <p>
            Condensing heaters, like the Rinnai RX160iN and Rheem RTGH, add a second heat exchanger that recovers heat from the exhaust. That pushes efficiency up to listed UEFs of 0.93 to 0.97. The exhaust is cool enough for plastic vent pipe, but the process creates acidic condensate that needs a drain.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Non-Condensing Units</h3>

          <p>
            Non-condensing heaters, like the Rinnai RE199iN and Rheem RTG outdoor model, are simpler and cheaper to buy, with no condensate drain. Their hot exhaust needs high-temperature venting indoors, and efficiency is lower, such as the Rheem RTG's listed 0.82 UEF.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which to Choose</h3>

          <p>
            If you will keep the heater for many years and pay for gas, condensing usually wins on running costs. If the purchase price or a missing drain location is the constraint, non-condensing can make sense. Our comparison of <a href="/condensing-vs-non-condensing-tankless-water-heater/">condensing vs non-condensing tankless water heaters</a> goes further.
          </p>

