        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Size Gas Tankless Water Heater Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tankless heaters are sized by flow rate at a given temperature rise, not by gallons of storage. Getting this right decides whether showers stay hot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Add Up Peak Flow</h3>

          <p>
            List the fixtures that might run at once. A shower typically uses about 2 gallons per minute, a kitchen faucet 1.5 to 2, and a dishwasher or washing machine 1 to 2. Two showers and a dishwasher come to roughly 5 to 6 GPM.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Find Your Temperature Rise</h3>

          <p>
            Temperature rise is the difference between incoming groundwater and the temperature you want, usually around 120°F. Groundwater can be in the 70s in the South and in the 40s in the North, so the rise ranges from about 45°F to 75°F. Rinnai publishes a groundwater temperature map for this.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Read Flow at Your Rise</h3>

          <p>
            Rheem rates its units at a 35°F rise, a warm-climate figure. At a 70°F rise the same heater delivers roughly half that flow. In cold climates, choose a 199k BTU unit; in warm ones, a 140k to 160k BTU unit is often enough. Our guide to <a href="/what-size-tankless-water-heater-do-i-need/">what size tankless water heater you need</a> has worked examples.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Installing a Gas Tankless Water Heater: Gas Line, Venting and Power
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every maker here recommends professional installation, and for good reason: gas, venting and combustion safety are involved. Knowing the requirements helps you budget and talk to installers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas Supply</h3>

          <p>
            A 199,000 BTU heater needs far more gas when firing than a typical tank heater. Many homes need a larger gas line from the meter, and sometimes a bigger meter, to supply it alongside a furnace and range. Your installer should size the line.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Venting</h3>

          <p>
            Indoor units need venting to the outside: plastic pipe for most condensing units, high-temperature pipe for non-condensing ones. Outdoor units, like the Rheem RTG outdoor model, avoid indoor venting. The Rinnai RX160iN can go either way with the right vent kit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Power, Drain and Permits</h3>

          <p>
            You need a 120-volt outlet, a condensate drain for condensing units, and in most areas a permit and inspection. Carbon monoxide alarms near sleeping areas are essential with any fuel-burning appliance. Our guide to <a href="/replace-water-heater-with-tankless/">replacing a tank water heater with tankless</a> covers the conversion.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Gas Tankless Water Heater Cost and Savings
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gas tankless heaters cost more up front than tank heaters, and the installation is often the larger part of the bill.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Purchase and Installation</h3>

          <p>
            The unit is only part of the cost. A gas line upgrade, new venting, a condensate drain and permits can add significantly, especially when converting from a tank. Our guide to <a href="/tankless-water-heater-cost/">tankless water heater cost</a> breaks down the typical items.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Running Cost</h3>

          <p>
            Savings come from not keeping a tank hot around the clock. Rheem says its units use up to 34 percent less energy than a 50-gallon electric tank. How much you save depends on your gas price, hot water use and the unit's efficiency, which is why condensing models with higher UEFs save more.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lifespan</h3>

          <p>
            Tankless heaters generally last longer than tank heaters, and the 15-year heat exchanger warranties from Rinnai and Rheem reflect that. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-tankless-water-heaters-last/">how long tankless water heaters last</a> covers what shortens their life.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Maintenance: Descaling and Care
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tankless heaters need little routine care, but they do need descaling, especially in hard water areas.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Scale Matters</h3>

          <p>
            Minerals in hard water build up inside the heat exchanger, reducing efficiency and flow and eventually triggering error codes. A yearly flush with a descaling solution is the usual recommendation in hard water areas.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Built-In Help</h3>

          <p>
            The Rinnai RX160iN has a Quick Flush feature, the RE199iN shows service reminders on its display, and Eccotemp sells an EZ-Flush descaling kit for its SMARTHOME series. Isolation valves on the water lines make flushing far easier.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Flush</h3>

          <p>
            Our guide to <a href="/how-to-flush-a-tankless-water-heater/">how to flush a tankless water heater</a> walks through the process. If your heater stops heating properly, see <a href="/tankless-water-heater-not-heating/">tankless water heater not heating</a> for the common causes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-tank" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Gas Tankless vs Gas Tank Water Heater
          </h2>

          <p>
            A gas tank water heater is still the right choice for some homes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Tankless Wins</h3>

          <p>
            Endless hot water for back-to-back showers, lower standby energy use, a much smaller footprint and a longer expected life. For households that run out of hot water with a tank, tankless solves the problem permanently.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where a Tank Wins</h3>

          <p>
            Lower purchase and installation cost, no gas line upgrade in most homes, and hot water stored for a short power outage. Our guide to the <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">best gas water heaters</a> covers tank models.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Deciding Questions</h3>

          <p>
            If you plan to stay in the home for many years and your gas line can support a tankless unit, tankless usually pays off. If you are on a tight budget or moving soon, a tank is simpler. Our <a href="/tankless-water-heater-pros-and-cons/">tankless pros and cons</a> guide covers the full picture.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="indoor-outdoor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Indoor vs Outdoor Gas Tankless Water Heaters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Where the heater goes affects installation cost, efficiency and how it copes with winter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Indoor Units</h3>

          <p>
            Indoor units, like the Rinnai RE199iN and Rheem RTGH, mount in a garage, basement or utility room and vent through a wall or roof. They are protected from weather, which makes them the standard choice in cold climates, but the vent run adds cost and limits where they can go.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Outdoor Units</h3>

          <p>
            Outdoor units, like the Rheem RTG outdoor model, mount on an exterior wall and need no vent pipe through the house. They free up indoor space and simplify installation, and they are common in mild climates. Their freeze protection relies on electricity, so a long outage in hard frost is a risk.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Flexible Units</h3>

          <p>
            Some heaters can go either way. The Rinnai RX160iN installs indoors or outdoors with the right vent kit, and the Noritz EZ Series Pro is listed for indoor or outdoor use. That flexibility helps when the obvious indoor spot is awkward to vent.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hot-water-wait" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. The Wait for Hot Water: Recirculation Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            A tankless heater does not make hot water reach the tap faster. The cold water sitting in the pipes still has to run out first, and some people notice a slightly longer wait than with a tank.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the Wait Happens</h3>

          <p>
            When you open a tap, the heater needs a moment to sense flow and fire, and then hot water has to travel down the pipe. The farther the tap is from the heater, the longer the wait and the more water goes down the drain.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Built-In Recirculation</h3>

          <p>
            The Rinnai REP199iN has a built-in recirculation pump that keeps hot water near the fixtures. Its Smart-Circ system learns when you use hot water and runs the pump on that schedule, rather than all day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Add-On Options</h3>

          <p>
            If you choose a heater without recirculation, a separate recirculation system or a dedicated return line can be added. Putting the heater near the bathrooms you use most is the simplest fix of all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing by the Biggest GPM Number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Flow ratings are often given at a 35°F temperature rise, a warm-climate figure. In a cold region where water must be heated 70°F, the same heater delivers roughly half that. Size by flow at your actual rise, using a groundwater temperature map.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Gas Line</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 199,000 BTU tankless heater draws far more gas than a tank heater when firing. An undersized gas line causes lukewarm water and error codes. Have the installer check the line and meter before you choose a model.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing the Wrong Venting for the Unit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Condensing units use plastic vent pipe and need a condensate drain; non-condensing units need high-temperature venting. Reusing an old tank heater's vent is usually not allowed. Price the correct venting into the decision.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Annual Descaling in Hard Water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Scale inside the heat exchanger reduces efficiency and flow and shortens the heater's life. Flush it yearly in hard water areas, and fit isolation valves at installation so flushing is easy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting It Needs Power</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gas tankless heaters need electricity to run. In an outage there is no hot water, and freeze protection on outdoor units stops working. If outages are common where you live, plan backup power or consider that a tank stores some hot water.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

