        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best gas tankless water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Rinnai RX160iN is the best gas tankless water heater for most homes. It is a condensing unit listed at a UEF of 0.97 and up to 9 GPM, runs on natural gas or propane, and installs indoors or outdoors. The Rinnai RE199iN is the better value for high flow, and the Noritz EZ Series Pro lists the most flow at 11.1 GPM.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do gas tankless water heaters need electricity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Gas tankless heaters use electricity for their controls, electronic ignition, combustion fan and freeze protection, usually from a standard 120-volt outlet. Without power, they cannot make hot water, so a gas tankless heater will not work in a power outage unless it has a backup power source.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are tankless water heaters gas or electric?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both types exist. Gas tankless heaters, running on natural gas or propane, deliver more hot water and are the usual choice for whole-house use, especially in cold climates. Electric tankless heaters suit smaller loads, point-of-use installs and homes without gas, but whole-house electric units need very large electrical service.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size gas tankless water heater do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Add up the flow of fixtures you use at once, then check each heater's flow at your temperature rise. Two showers and a dishwasher need about 5 to 6 GPM. In cold climates with a 70°F rise, that usually means a 199,000 BTU unit; in warm climates, 140,000 to 160,000 BTU is often enough.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are gas tankless water heaters worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For households that run out of hot water, plan to stay for many years and have a gas supply that can support one, they usually are. They provide endless hot water, use less standby energy and last longer than tanks. The catch is higher installation cost, especially when a gas line upgrade or new venting is needed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do gas tankless water heaters last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Gas tankless heaters generally last longer than tank heaters when they are descaled regularly. Rinnai and Rheem list 15-year warranties on the heat exchanger for the models here, which reflects their expected life. Hard water without descaling is the main thing that shortens it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a gas tankless water heater myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Every maker here recommends professional installation, and Rinnai says it does not recommend installing without a technician. The job involves gas line sizing, venting and combustion safety, usually needs a permit, and DIY installation can affect the warranty. Hire a licensed installer.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
